nd shop for gasoline I've not been  going just being aware  what has been going on and it's really  been like a major major news story we  learned this morning that it appears as  though they are very close to reaching  an agreement which will get employees  back to work and it'll get the stores  reopened and that's all you know  hopefully it gets resolved in a way that  is actually good for workers but there's  a couple funny stories that are going  along with it I'll tell you the one  about my mom in a second one is that  there's another supermarket chain in the  area called Big Y which as far as I know  and like former producer Louis worked  there when we were in high school at big  Y and many other people I know have  worked there over time as far as I know  they're extraordinarily anti-union so  anti-union that they have actually  successfully suppressed unions for  decades at big Y so you have this kind  of like a cute union related thing going  on it's stop and shop and a lot of  people are saying rightly I'm not going  to cross the picket line and then they  go to Big Y which has is it's so  anti-union that there's just not even a  chance that any kind of union would  develop at big why what's the right  thing to do I don't actually know I  don't know the answer I know that  crossing the picket line when there are  striking workers is not good but then to  say well I'm gonna go and give money to  the place that has succeeded in  suppressing unions for decades that also  doesn't really feel like the right thing  so what what I've been doing is just  continuing to try to go to local grocery  stores to the extent that they exist  aside from the stop and shop strike and  I do sometimes go to Whole Foods which  was against Obamacare now Whole Foods is  owned by Amazon what you know I don't  know I I feel like it's a very difficult  situation for consumers particularly  when a lot of the local grocery stores  where I live are quite frankly not good  but the situation involving my mom is  she somehow didn't really know what was  going on and she told me last week that  she went to a stop and shop and saw that  it was mostly empty and that there were  tons of employees outside she didn't  really know what was going on and she  just went in and did her shopping and  then only later learned what was going  on and I said mom you crossed the picket  line and you support unions and you  support workers and she felt really  really bad and said she would go to Big  Y instead at which  I explained to her you know big big y is  really anti-union  and they have been successfully so for  for a very long time so I don't know  tell me what you think what is the right  thing to do in these cases you know and  at a certain point it becomes an a  common example is you don't want to help  the the oil industry by having a car and  purchasing gasoline but at the same time  you need the car to do your activism to  mitigate the effects of climate change  how do you weigh it is your activism  worth the patronizing of oil companies  and burning of fossil fuels because  you're trying to fix the problems we're  creating with fossil fuels how does  anyone make that calculation I genuinely  don't know and that applies to this show  as well right we have a carbon footprint  I travel to conferences where I  interview newsmakers that as a carbon  footprint should I just sit in a room in  the dark to not use lights and  electricity what's the level to which  each of us is comfortable saying this is  a valuable exchange based on what we're  getting out of it I don't know if  there's any simple answers to it but I'm  curious to hear from you if you follow  me on instagram at david pakman you  might have seen that i posted a picture  wearing a yarmulke on saturday from a  passover seder drinking wine not  Manischewitz  better wine i will make very clear I  went to a Passover Seder as I do most  years and a lot of people very angrily  saying David how can you do this I  thought you were an atheist I thought  you were agnostic and you're wearing a  yarmulke and attending a seder which is  a religious event it's your traitorous  and blah-blah-blah-blah-blah  and not that I was starting to argue  with people on Instagram but a bunch of  my friends wrote to me and they said I  saw what you posted and I saw all these  angry people don't they understand  cultural Judaism and the answer is a lot  of people don't I mean in the same way  that a lot of people do presents around  Christmas or I don't know do an Easter  egg hunt around  Easter without literally believing that  Jesus Christ had been resurrected at  that point in time for the sort of  family and community and traditional  reasons with Judaism I don't remember  what percentage but I think it's like  75% of Jews or something like that  consider themselves Jewish but secular  in the sense that they are part of the  Jewish culture many of them are  ethnically Jewish Jewish yes that's a  thing you can look it up and check out  my DNA test without literally believing  that God was choosing which houses to  pass over based on whether they had been  painted with I don't remember even if it  was supposedly blood or whatever knowing  not to kill the firstborn of the people  within those homes I don't believe any  of that stuff as people know but I  identify culturally as Jewish I do very  very little I mean Hanukkah I might  light candles one or two of the nights  if I'm home and my girlfriend remembers  but Passover is actually one that I do  enjoy getting to see people I don't see  most of the year and having some of the  foods and the hérault sit with the matzo  and the entire thing so please don't be  angry with me for participating in what  for me is a cultural thing I don't have  the religious beliefs  some people do and I'm glad to debate  with them the truth or falsity of those  religious beliefs and whether they are  backed by science or history and I  believe that they are not but I actually  had one person right and said say that  they were cancelling their membership  because they can't support in good faith  anyone who follows religious dogma it  could have been one of these  unsubscribed trolls who's not even  actually a member  although I don't know their Instagram  username did not indicate to me their  real name therefore I couldn't actually  look up whether they were or were not a  member but come on come on cultural  people celebrate Christmas without  believing the Christian Jesus story  people celebrate Easter people celebrate  all sorts of stuff and I had a lovely  Passover by the way I will say matzo  ball soup fantastic some of the other  food you know so so so but overall  a great-great Passover and thanks to  everybody who actually said hope you had  a nice time David and didn't drink too  much wine which I didn't I really don't  I I don't I don't drink a lot ever so  just add a little bit of red wine that  does it for me I am out of town as many  of you can see and notice the remote  setup continues to improve I actually  have people who write to me and they say  David when your remote the lighting is  almost better because I have very nice  front lighting the background is further  in the distance which creates more  separation between me in the background  I have people who say they love the  lighting when I'm remote  although the audio sometimes people  don't like as much it is an inferior  microphone I have an sm58 that I bring  with me which is like a I think it's  like a hundred and twenty dollar  microphone very popular mic for voice  but it is not an e V re 20 which is what  we have in the studio and it is a much  more expensive microphone so I guess the  Lighting's good the sound may be not  quite as good or at least different but  I'm continuing to work on it and  tomorrow should be even better  the david pakman show at david  pakman.com  [Music]

